---
title: The Picture of Dorian Gray
subject: Oscar Wilde
genre: Books
rating: 4
cover: /posters/the-picture-of-dorian-gray.jpg
date: 2026-09-02
excerpt: >-
  A chase for pleasure, hiding away the parts of him he found ugly, but
  ultimately meeting his demise at his own hands.
---

As Dorian Gray took his final breath and clattered onto the cold hard floor alone, and the portrait of his, in all its beauty as a snapshot in time, looks back at the lifeless body, there's a sense of irony in how Wilde paints the picture. Dorian, who represents this yearning for hedonism, tries one more time to find pleasure in his life by destroying the one ugly piece in his life, ultimately finds his demise by attempting to butcher the real version of his likeness. How does a life built around hedonism and vanity corrupt a man? Throughout the book, Dorian Gray is placed at the center of two ideals represented by Lord Henry and Basil. Henry embodies self indulgence, this pining for pleasure and to be liked by society. He believes in this idea of youth and appearance as a metric for social standing. His interactions with Dorian, both literally in terms of giving him the book that Dorian calls poison, and emotionally, from his words and actions, show the reader in real time how he taints and morphs Dorian. It's clear how much Dorian is fighting his own moral conscience, especially in the first half of the book. Henry represents this cynical, nihilistic view. Yet, quite ironically, this devil-like soulless man is the one who quotes Hamlet and asks, "what does it profit a man if he gain the whole world and lose his own soul?" This is the crux of Dorian's character and through his actions, we see how he evolves and regresses. Wilde explores how Sibyl's death affects both parts of Dorian's conscience and how he struggles to reconcile until Henry affirms his beliefs. It feels as though we are witnessing two separate characters, each speaking and thinking in separate ways. In one chapter Dorian resolves to never see Henry again, that his life with Sibyl will be beautiful and pure. One chapter later, sitting beside Henry, he asks "why is it that I cannot feel this tragedy as much as I want to?" One yearns for a beautiful life with her; the other is cold, calculated, and concerned with his own optics more than a human life. This back and forth is what makes Dorian's descent, and ultimate demise, much more powerful. It's one thing when an author uses power as a tool to corrupt and we see the ramifications immediately but it's another where an author uses vanity as a tool and it slowly bleeds through, just as the painting shows its cracks one by one.

![Napoleon Sarony's 1882 studio portrait of Oscar Wilde](/posters/the-picture-of-dorian-gray-1.jpg)

Basil, the creator of the painting, represents a sense of admiration, of good conscience, and of the value of art in conjunction with real beauty. His berating of Dorian Gray is a powerful moment because it forces Dorian to make a choice, to pick a side, to conclude to himself, and to the readers, if he truly has fallen, and there is no line that proves this more than when Wilde explains that to Dorian, "evil was a mode by which he could realize conception of beauty." The chapter in which Basil dies shows the reader this fall for Dorian in something as simple as the wording in how Basil is described. Beginning the chapter, Wilde mentions him by name, then following the act, simply refers to him as the "man," and finally closing the chapter by describing it as a hideous "thing," epitomizing how quickly Dorian dehumanizes someone through just the prose. The painting that Basil creates functions as this third personality and third representation of Dorian, and this time it's the true version. Wilde expertly places this painting in Dorian's childhood home, further driving the point home of this painting being a traumatic reflection of who he really is. Dorian's disdain for it is highlighted by his comparison to his grandfather and the hideous and veiny bulges that he finds distasteful. It's ironic how the painting that was so beautifully envisioned touches on all three characters. It's admiration and the soul for Basil, the hedonistic chase and nihilistic outlook for Henry, and the envy and eventual path to death for Dorian. The placement of the painting, the comparisons Dorian makes, and the eventual repercussions of it leading to Dorian's death, perfectly paint what his life was. A chase for pleasure, hiding away the parts of him he found ugly, but ultimately meeting his demise at his own hands.

![Dorian dead beneath the restored portrait: wood engraving by Eugene Dete after Paul Thiriat, from the 1908 illustrated edition](/posters/the-picture-of-dorian-gray-2.jpg)

Wilde introduces other characters which all form around Dorian, like the Duchess, whose sole purpose was to explore how intoxicating it must have been to be around him. He explores James Vane as grief and vengeance, the physical manifestation of Dorian's consequences. When Dorian faints at the near miss in the hunting party, it's the clearest sign that beneath everything, he still doesn't want to die. Even by the end Dorian didn't become entirely nihilistic and hedonistic. He was still fearful of death, and I believe this was the last bit of the painting that hadn't been taken from him. And as the book closes, we see one last time, the painting of the pure Dorian Gray staring back at a face without a heart.
